  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "Determine the value of the following series rounded to $3$ **decimal places.**\n\n${\\sum }_{n=0}^{5}5{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n-1}\u2248$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Since the given series is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. Notice that the index starts from $n=0$ instead of $n=1.$\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{0}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Also, note that $N=6,$ since there are $6$ terms in total.\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=5{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nand so the first term ${a}_{0}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =5{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{-1} \\\\  & =5(\\frac{3}{1}) \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{0}=15,r=\\frac{1}{3},N=6$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{6} & =15\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{6}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =15\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{6}]}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{2}\\cdot [1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{6}] \\\\  & \u224822.469\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the final answer has been rounded to three decimal places.",
